WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Safetensors, pickle, and running someone else’s code.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.235
For years, PyTorch weights were saved with Python’s pickle format.

3
00:00:07.515 --> 00:00:15.344
Hugging Face’s security docs explain the problem plainly: unpickling can execute arbitrary code, so loading a malicious.

4
00:00:15.624 --> 00:00:18.845
bin file can run whatever its author wanted on your machine.

5
00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:23.883
The Hub scans pickles and shows their imports, but the real fix was a new format.

6
00:00:24.163 --> 00:00:26.744
Safetensors stores tensors and nothing else.

7
00:00:27.024 --> 00:00:43.216
The format, documented in the project README, is simple enough to read with the standard library: the first 8 bytes are an unsigned little-endian 64-bit integer giving the length of a JSON header; the header maps each tensor name to its dtype, shape,

8
00:00:43.496 --> 00:00:49.619
and byte offsets in the buffer that follows; an optional __metadata__ entry holds string-to-string metadata.

9
00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:59.115
There is no code to execute, files can be memory-mapped, and a loader can read just the header to learn the model’s shapes, or load one tensor without the rest.

10
00:00:59.395 --> 00:01:16.035
In 2023 Hugging Face, EleutherAI, and Stability AI commissioned an external security audit of the library (performed by Trail of Bits) and said its results let them move toward making safetensors the default format for saved models;

11
00:01:16.315 --> 00:01:20.603
today it is the format you will find in nearly every major model repo.

12
00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:23.784
Code execution can still come in through the front door.

13
00:01:24.064 --> 00:01:33.408
Models with custom architectures ship their own Python modeling files, and loading them requires trust_remote_code=True, which runs that code.

14
00:01:33.688 --> 00:01:44.163
The transformers docs advise extra caution and recommend loading such models from a specific commit hash with revision=, so the code you reviewed can’t change.

15
00:01:44.443 --> 00:01:53.552
Make that a general rule: pin revisions in anything you deploy, prefer safetensors files, and treat trust_remote_code as a code review request.
